<!--
    kuna `strlenscan`: gcc 9 expands strlen inline as
    `mov rcx,-1; mov eax,0; repnz scasb; not rcx`, and SLEIGH lifts the repeat
    prefix as a loop inside the one instruction, so the C showed
    `do { if (!v3) break; v3 -= 1; ... } while (v2);` and `~v3 - 1` where
    the source has `strlen(s)`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P3 "simplification-quiescence".  The P0 assertion
    `option strlenscan on|off` decides whether the proved scan becomes the
    `strlen` builtin, which is ordered like a call: it stays where the scan
    was, ahead of a store or a call that follows, and is not merged across a
    join.

    The bytes are functions of decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/
    strlenscan.c built by Ubuntu 20.04's gcc 9.4 at -O1 (x86-64 SysV): name_len,
    last_char and weighted_len (lengths), len_then_clear and len_then_wipe (a
    length read after a store and after a call that change the string),
    add_slash (`strcat(path, "/")` inlined as a scan and two byte stores),
    len_join (one scan on each side of a branch, the length read after the
    join), and scan_slash and raw_count (scans that are no string length).

    Pass 1 runs with no option line, so it pins the shipped default.  Pass 2
    (`option strlenscan off`) reproduces the bug: every scan is a loop.
-->
